flowchart TB

  subgraph INTRO["READ ME FIRST"]
    direction TB
    P0["`**What the paper does**
    Image-recognition AIs are usually trained
    with a standard method called cross-entropy:
    show a photo, reward the correct label.
    This paper proposes SupCon. First the AI learns
    to place photos of the same class close together
    and different classes far apart. Then a simple
    label-reading layer is added on top.
    The authors claim it is more accurate,
    more robust and easier to tune.`"]
    HOW["`**How to read this chart**
    Read each section from top to bottom.
    Blue boxes on top = evidence from the paper.
    Rounded boxes below = what the authors claim.
    Arrow label = how well the evidence backs the claim.
    Dotted arrow = the evidence goes AGAINST the claim.
    Grey six-sided boxes at the bottom = unstated
    assumptions the claim quietly relies on.`"]
    GL["`**Key terms**
    Cross-entropy (CE): the standard training method
    SupCon: the new method proposed in this paper
    Top-1 accuracy: % of photos where the first guess is right
    ImageNet: about 1.3 million photos in 1000 classes
    ResNet-50, ResNet-200: standard AI models, small and large
    ImageNet-C: ImageNet photos damaged with blur, noise, fog
    Corruption error (mCE): error on damaged photos, lower is better
    Calibration error: gap between confidence and real accuracy
    Training settings: hand-made choices like optimizer or learning rate
    Temperature: a SupCon-only setting for how sharply classes separate
    Gradient: the signal that tells the AI how to adjust itself
    Hard example: a photo the AI currently finds confusing`"]
    subgraph LEG["Colour key for claims"]
      direction LR
      K1("Supported")
      K2("Partly supported")
      K3("Weakly supported")
      K4("Not supported or contradicted")
      K5["Evidence"]
      K6{{"Hidden assumption"}}
    end
    P0 ~~~ HOW ~~~ GL ~~~ LEG
  end

  subgraph T1["QUESTION 1: Is SupCon more accurate than the standard method?"]
    E1["`**E1. Table 2**
    ResNet-50, one run each. SupCon beats CE by
    1.0 (CIFAR-10), 1.2 (CIFAR-100)
    and 0.5 points (ImageNet)`"]
    E2["`**E2. Table 3**
    ImageNet vs the authors' own CE:
    +1.1 points (ResNet-50), +0.5 (ResNet-200).
    One run each, no error bars`"]
    E19["`**E19. Fig. 7**
    SupCon beats CE at every
    augmentation strength tried
    (judged from the caption only)`"]
    E4["`**E4. Section 4.1**
    CE with a 2x bigger batch (77.5%)
    or 1400 epochs (77.0%) still
    stays below SupCon (78.7%)`"]
    E5["`**E5. Table 7**
    CE trained in two stages
    like SupCon does worse (73.7%)`"]
    E20["`**E20. Several tables**
    The same CE ResNet-50 baseline
    is reported as 77.1, 77.6, 78.2
    and 78.24% in different places`"]

    C8("`**C8. Gain is not just bigger batches
    or longer training**
    Verdict: partly supported`")
    C9("`**C9. Gain is not just
    the two-stage recipe**
    Verdict: partly supported`")
    C7("`**C7. New record for ResNet-50: 78.7%**
    Only 0.1 points above the
    best published CE result
    Verdict: weakly supported`")
    C1("`**C1. MAIN CLAIM: More accurate than CE**
    on every dataset and model size tested
    Verdict: partly supported`")
    C2("`**C2. MAIN CLAIM: Record 81.4% on ResNet-200**
    0.8 points above published CE, but only 0.5
    above CE trained with the same augmentation
    Verdict: weakly supported`")

    A1{{"`One run per setting is precise
    enough to trust differences
    of 0.5 to 1 point`"}}
    A2{{"`CE got equally careful tuning
    and equal training effort`"}}
    A4{{"`Settings were not chosen by
    looking at the same test scores
    that are reported`"}}
    GAP["`**BIGGEST GAP IN THE PAPER**
    The gains are 0.5 to 1.1 points,
    each from a single run.
    The paper's own CE baseline shifts
    by 1.1 points between tables.
    So part of the gain may be noise,
    though its consistent direction helps.`"]

    E1 ~~~ E4
    E2 ~~~ E5
    E19 ~~~ E20
  end

  subgraph T2["QUESTION 2: Does it cope better with damaged photos?"]
    E6["`**E6. Fig. 3 (table)**
    On ImageNet-C, corruption error drops
    by 1.4 (ResNet-50) and 1.8 points (ResNet-200)`"]
    E7["`**E7. Table 6**
    SupCon's accuracy lead grows with damage:
    0.6 to 1.5 points (ResNet-50),
    0.6 to 2.6 points (ResNet-200)`"]
    E8["`**E8. Table 6**
    SupCon's calibration error rises with damage
    (0.024 to 0.100), while CE ResNet-200
    barely rises (0.045 to 0.052)`"]
    E5b["`**E5. Table 7 (robustness columns)**
    Two-stage CE has a better relative
    corruption error (92.9 vs 94.6)`"]
    C11("`**C11. Confidence stays honest
    under damage, unlike CE**
    The paper's own table shows the opposite
    Verdict: contradicted`")
    C3("`**C3. MAIN CLAIM: More robust to damaged photos**
    Holds for accuracy, mixed for the relative measure
    Verdict: partly supported`")
    A5{{"`Lower error on damaged photos is not
    just a side effect of higher accuracy
    on clean photos`"}}
  end

  subgraph T3["QUESTION 3: Is it less fussy about training settings?"]
    E9["`**E9. Fig. 4a**
    Changing augmentation, optimizer
    or learning rate moves SupCon's
    accuracy visibly less than CE's
    (runs per box not reported)`"]
    E11["`**E11. Tables 8 and 9**
    Within SupCon alone, changing
    optimizer or augmentation moves
    accuracy by 2.6 to 3.2 points`"]
    E12["`**E12. Fig. 4d, Supplement 10**
    The SupCon-only temperature
    setting changes accuracy
    by nearly 3%`"]
    C4("`**C4. MAIN CLAIM: Less sensitive to training settings**
    True only for the settings varied in Fig. 4a
    Verdict: partly supported`")
    A3{{"`The settings varied in Fig. 4a
    are the ones that matter in practice`"}}
  end

  subgraph T4["QUESTION 4: Why does it work? (design and theory)"]
    E13["`**E13. Table 1**
    Loss version L-out: 78.7%
    Version L-in: 67.4%
    An 11.3-point gap, one run each`"]
    E14["`**E14. Section 3.2.2**
    Math argument: one loss is an
    upper bound of the other, plus a
    gradient argument for a special case`"]
    E15["`**E15. Supplement 7**
    Derivation: hard examples
    produce larger gradients,
    under stated conditions`"]
    E16["`**E16. Table 5**
    More same-class photos per anchor
    raises accuracy from 69.3% (one)
    to 78.5% (no limit)`"]
    C15("`**C15. Theory explains why it is better**
    An upper bound does not imply
    a better training target
    Verdict: weakly supported`")
    C10("`**C10. More same-class photos
    per anchor = better**
    Verdict: partly supported`")
    C5("`**C5. MAIN CLAIM: The chosen loss version
    is much better than the obvious alternative**
    Verdict: supported`")
    C6("`**C6. MAIN CLAIM: Automatically
    focuses on hard examples**
    Shown by math, not measured in training
    Verdict: partly supported`")
  end

